      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, we start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\]\n\nFirst, simplify each expression:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNext, we need to eliminate the parameter \\(t\\) and find a relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). Start by expressing \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\) in terms of \\(x\\):\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\]\n\nNow substitute \\(\\frac{1}{t}\\) in the expression for \\(y\\):\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - (x - 1) \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - x + 1 \\]\n\\[ y = 2 - x \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a straight line. Therefore, the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
