      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie for \\(t \\neq 0\\), we can express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\) and then find the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nFrom these equations, we can solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\):\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\]\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - y \\implies t = \\frac{1}{1 - y} \\]\n\nSince both expressions equal \\(t\\), we can set them equal to each other:\n\\[ \\frac{1}{x - 1} = \\frac{1}{1 - y} \\]\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[ 1 - y = x - 1 \\]\n\\[ x + y = 2 \\]\n\nThus, all the points \\((x, y)\\) satisfy the equation \\(x + y = 2\\), which is the equation of a line.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{A}\n\\]",
