      "text": "To determine the type of curve on which all the points \\((x, y)\\) lie, we need to express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\) and then eliminate the parameter \\(t\\).\n\nGiven the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\), we can start by expressing \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ y = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\nNow, we need to eliminate the parameter \\(t\\). We can do this by expressing \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and then substituting it into the equation for \\(y\\).\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\\[ x = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{1}{t} = x - 1 \\]\n\\[ t = \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\]\n\nNow substitute \\(t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\\) into the second equation:\n\\[ y = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} = 1 - (x - 1) = 1 - x + 1 = 2 - x \\]\n\nSo, the relationship between \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) is:\n\\[ y = 2 - x \\]\n\nThis is the equation of a line. Therefore, all the plotted points \\((x, y)\\) lie on a line.\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{A}\\).",
