      "text": "We need to determine the equation of the curve on which the points \\((x, y) = \\left( \\frac{t + 1}{t}, \\frac{t - 1}{t} \\right)\\) lie, where \\(t \\neq 0\\).\n\nFirst, let's express \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in terms of \\(t\\):\n\\[\nx = \\frac{t + 1}{t} = 1 + \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\n\\[\ny = \\frac{t - 1}{t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{t}\n\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(t\\) in terms of \\(x\\) and \\(y\\). From the expression for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx = 1 + \\frac{1}{t} \\implies x - 1 = \\frac{1}{t} \\implies t = \\frac{1}{x - 1}\n\\]\n\nFrom the expression for \\(y\\):\n\\[\ny = 1 - \\frac{1}{t} \\implies y - 1 = -\\frac{1}{t} \\implies t = -\\frac{1}{y - 1}\n\\]\n\nSince both expressions for \\(t\\) must be equal, we set them equal to each other:\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x - 1} = -\\frac{1}{y - 1}\n\\]\n\nCross-multiplying gives:\n\\[\n1 \\cdot (y - 1) = -1 \\cdot (x - 1) \\implies y - 1 = -x + 1 \\implies y + x = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the points \\((x, y)\\) lie on the line \\(x + y = 2\\).\n\nThe correct option is \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
